{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7K5SHZogbGYh7FfuSwz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft this, I have five questions. The answers will change what the email says.\n\n1. **Dates.** Today is 16 November 2026, so I'm reading \"September 1\" as 1 September 2027. Does \"until the end of next year\" mean 31 December 2027, or the end of 2028? If it's December 2027, the four long-distance staff get only four months longer than everyone else. I'd write that differently from a full extra year.\n\n2. **The other 11 remote hires.** They're within two hours of the office but were hired as fully remote. Is the firm offering them anything, such as help with travel costs, more time to adjust, or a way to ask for an exception for caregiving or health reasons? If not, should the email say so plainly?\n\n3. **Talking to people first.** Will the 15 remote hires, especially the 4, hear about this from a partner before the email goes out? If they will, the email can mention it. If they won't, it's worth considering, because they're the people most likely to feel a promise has been broken.\n\n4. **Mondays and Fridays.** Are those still open for remote work, or up to each person? Is there anything else people will want to know, like desks, parking, or core hours?\n\n5. **Sign-off.** Should it come from all three partners by name, or from you?\n\nIf you don't know the answer to any of these, say so and I'll write the email without that part."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":255,"cache_read_input_tokens":9539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":255},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"277db71c-afdf-41